7581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1783 0 R
/E (You're spreading your marketing budget across too many audiences and seeing weak results everywhere. Or you're about to launch a new product and need to decide which customer segment to go after first.)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
7582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1783 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
7583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1783 0 R
/E (Developed by Philip Kotler, the STP Model is a three-stage strategic marketing process. Segmentation divides the total market into distinct groups with different needs or behaviours. Targeting evaluates each segment and chooses which to focus on,  based on size, growth, accessibility, and fit with your capabilities. Positioning then defines how you want to be perceived by the chosen segment relative to competitors. STP prevents the costly mistake of trying to be everything to everyone.)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
7584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 12674 0 R
/E <4D617020796F7572206D61726B657420696E746F206174206C65617374203485362064697374696E6374207365676D656E7473206265666F7265206576616C756174696E6720776869636820746F207461726765742E>
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
7585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 12675 0 R
/E (Score each segment on attractiveness and your ability to win in it,  not just size.)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
7586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 12676 0 R
/E (For the chosen target segment, write a positioning statement using Framework 3.7.)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
7587 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 12677 0 R
/E (Resist targeting more than two segments until you have fully won the first.)
/Pg 95 0 R
/K [11]
>>
